  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nMidlevel flow pattern is dominated by 600 dam high over the southern \nAppalachians. A few areas of enhanced moisture are noted along the \nN/NW periphery of this circulation, although none are resulting in \nimpacts to the sensible weather over the UP at this time. This is \ndue to high pressure building into Lake Superior/the UP from the \nnorthwest, leading to a seasonable and pleasant day. A northern \nstream midlevel trough is pushing eastward across the southern \nPrairie Provinces, and will continue to do so tonight. This will \nshift the surface high east overnight, and teaming up with some of \nthe midlevel moisture noted over the central/northern Plains, brings \nthe potential for a few light showers overnight. This potential is \nbrief and non-impactful, as time-height sections indicate the \nmidlevel moisture exiting once saturation occurs at lower levels.\n\nMidlevel ridging, as an extension of the 600 dam high, will build \nover the northern Plains Thursday before flattening out on Friday \ndue to vort maxes ejecting eastward from a western US trough. This \nwill result in another surface high building from the northwest \nThursday, leading to another pleasant and seasonably mild day with \nhighs of 65-75. \n\nBy Friday, surface low associated with the ejecting vort maxes will \ndevelop over the central Plains and move slowly eastward through \nSaturday. Meanwhile, surface high will remain anchored over Ontario \nwhile slowly drifting eastward. Seasonably strong 30-40kt southerly \nLLJ with Gulf connections will be found over the Plains, resulting \nin healthy poleward theta-e advection. These ingredients point to a \nprolonged heavy rain potential due to overrunning at the nose of the \nLLJ, but also a sharp gradient in the precipitation shield due to \nthe dry airmass associated with the high. There is still \nconsiderable uncertainty in the track of the relevant features and \nthe swath of the heaviest rainfall. In a system-relative sense, the \nheaviest swath and resulting flooding concerns will be south of the \nUP, but there is still potential for beneficial rainfall amounts to \nalleviate existing drought conditions over roughly the southern half \nof the UP, where probabilities of exceeding an inch of rain are 40-\n60 percent. Some higher end totals cannot be totally ruled out, with \nthe 90pctl of the NBM showing 2.5-3.5 inch amounts; however, the \nflip side of this is the 10pctl is less than 0.25 inches. The window \nfor this rain is Friday afternoon into Saturday morning, although \nthere is moderate potential for some showers to linger into Saturday \nafternoon. A dry forecast is favored by Sunday as another high \nbuilds in from the north. Highs will cool back into the 60s for the \nFriday into the weekend period.",
